	// GetHealthReport Deep self-diagnosis of the installation
	//
	// The state of every dependency, the active feature restrictions, the backlogs, and the
	// configuration warnings. Deliberately answers `200` even when `status` is `degraded` -
	// the HTTP status describes whether the endpoint is reachable, the `status` field
	// describes the system. The internal `/meta/health` on the operations listener serves the
	// same report and answers `503` when the status is `down`, because a status page reads a
	// status code and holds no token; this route does not, for the reason above.
	//
	// **One route, two answers.** A credential holding `admin:tenants` is the installation's
	// operator and receives the whole report. Any other reader receives `status`, `version` and
	// `degraded_features` and nothing else: which features are degraded is a statement about
	// what the reader is about to try, while the dependency names, the backlogs, the migration
	// state and the configuration warnings are the installation's internals and do not cross a
	// tenant boundary. That reader needs the `ops:read` scope and the permission a workspace
	// administrator holds (`STRUCTURE`, or `READ_CONFIGURATION` for an auditor).
	//
	// The unauthenticated probes `/healthz`, `/startupz` and `/readyz` live outside `/api/v1`
	// and return only status codes.
