// GetAccountWithResponse The name behind an account identifier
//
// Every record that says *who* — an `ActivityEntry.actor`, an assignee, a member row — carries
// an identifier and no label, and says why: "the account is one request away and this record
// is deleted with its entry, so there is nothing for a copy of somebody's name to outlive".
// This is that request.
//
// **What comes back is deliberately less than `/accounts/me`.** The display name and the kind,
// and not the email, the locale, the time zone or the first day of the week. Those are the
// caller's own business or an administrator's; a name is what a workspace has to show to be
// readable at all. `data-protection.md` §9 settles the line: the visibility of profile data to
// other tenant members is *minimal — display name, avatar*, and this is that minimum.
//
// Any member of the tenant may make it. There is nothing narrower to authorise against: the
// identifiers a client holds arrived in records it was already allowed to read, and row level
// security bounds the answer to the caller's own tenant, so an identifier from elsewhere does
// not resolve here rather than being refused here.
//
// An erased account answers with the marker its erasure wrote where the name was, because that
// is what the row holds — the reading is not special-cased, and there is nothing left to leak.
