// ListMembershipsWithResponse Who holds a role at a scope
//
// The memberships granted **at** the scope named — an account or a group, a role, an
// identifier — and none granted elsewhere. A membership applies downwards, so what is in
// force at a collection is this list plus what its hub and the workspace grant; the client
// composes that path itself, because it knows the path it is on. An `effective` listing that
// walked it server-side is deliberately not offered until a second caller wants one.
//
// A row carries an identifier and no label, for the reason every record that says *who*
// does: the name is one request away (`GET /accounts/{accountId}`, `GET /groups/{groupId}`).
//
// Who may read is `READ` at the scope: a member of a hub seeing who else is in it is what
// makes a shared workspace legible, and `data-protection.md` §9 already limits what a name
// reveals. A scope the caller holds nothing on is not found, in the words a missing one
// produces (T-04). The workspace itself (`scope_type: TENANT`) is the one scope whose
// existence is no secret, so a refusal there is a refusal.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /memberships (the `ListMemberships` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListMembershipsWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ListMembershipsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListMembershipsResult, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListMemberships(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListMembershipsResult(rsp)
}

