// German word and an English one by a stemmed English word. The *query* is read under the
// caller's language, which is this request's `language` or the caller's locale. An entry that
// states no language is matched word by word and is found by any caller. Which languages this
// installation can index at all is answered by `GET /meta/capabilities` under
// `text_languages`; a language it has no configuration for searches word by word rather than
// failing.
//
// A script without word boundaries - Japanese, Chinese, Korean, Thai - additionally matches on
// substrings, because a run of such characters is one token and no word search can find part
// of it.
//
// Unanchored unless a container is named, and this is the one read of the API that is: it
// answers what the caller may see rather than refusing what they may not, so a page can be
// shorter than the size asked for. Walk on until `has_more` is false rather than stopping at
// the first short page. Naming a hub is the exception - a client that named a hub it cannot
// read is told so.
//
// A `POST` and no `GET`. What somebody is looking for is their content, and a query string
// travels through access logs, proxies and browser history; the same words in a body do not
// (security.md §9, ADR-0018). What is searched today is entries - tasks, work packages and
// activities - which is what `searchItems` says; a later milestone that makes containers or
// comments searchable widens this operation rather than adding a second one.
